# Research Documents

`context/research/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<slug>.md` records the results of one
research run (deepsearch, web/document sweep, spike) and its provenance. Read
this file when running or writing up research that feeds an analysis, proposal
or decision.

## Create

Create a new research note with the CLI (scaffolds the dated path and
frontmatter):

```bash
sdt context new --type research --title "<title>"
```

Timestamps (`created`/`updated`) come from `sdt time iso` (RFC3339 UTC).
Or hand-write the file from the template below.

## Contract

Every research note starts with frontmatter:

```yaml
kind: research
title: "One-line research title"
summary: "1-2 sentence index summary — MANDATORY"
subject: "The question this run answers"
context: "Why this research was requested"
status: draft # draft | active | archived — statuses defined in the matrix (architecture/stack.md)
created: "<ISO 8601>"
updated: "<ISO 8601>"
sources:            # provenance: the driving prompt + evidence refs
  - prompts/<driving-prompt>.md
  - refs/<evidence>.md
project: <project>
agent: <agent/tool>          # optional
model: <model id>            # optional
session: <session id>        # optional
```

- **No H1 title** — body starts at H2; see AGENTS.md (document conventions).

Body sections: Subject, Method, Findings, Evidence, Limits and open points,
Feeds.

## Purpose and boundary

- **Research = raw results, not a decision.** A research note gathers and
  organizes evidence (with links) so an analysis or proposal can reason over
  it — it never states an accepted choice.
- **Research feeds analyses and proposals.** Link forward with
  `links: analysis/…, proposals/…` once the consuming document exists; a
  proposal or decision cites the research in its `sources`.
- **Boundary vs analysis.** An analysis interprets evidence to reach a
  conclusion/recommendation; research merely collects and attributes it. When
  interpretation starts, write/extend an analysis (or proposal) instead.
- **Provenance is mandatory.** Every run names its driving prompt
  (`prompts/<…>.md`) and the evidence it relied on under `sources`.
  Non-markdown evidence is converted first with `sdt doc2md` (anydoc → docling
  → markitdown — see `instructions/ingestion.md`) and the original kept in
  `context/refs/`.
- **Web sources are captured, never fetched ad hoc.** A web/document sweep
  captures every page it relies on with `sdt crawldown` — stdout to read, a file
  under `context/refs/` to keep — and cites the capture; an uncaptured page is
  not evidence (see the AGENTS.md HARD RULES).

## Research runs (`sdt research`)

A research note summarises *a run*; the run itself is an auditable, machine-
readable artefact managed by the `sdt research` family. The two are distinct:
the **run** is transient working state; the **note** is the durable document.

### Run manifest

A run lives under `context/tmp/research/<run-id>/` and holds:

- `manifest.yaml` — the machine-readable record: `run_id`, `query`, `scope`,
  `objective` (the kebab-case grouping key naming the archive directory; derived
  from the query when `--objective` is omitted), `archive_dir` (set on first
  archive), `created`/`updated`, `tool`/`config`, `budget`
  (`max_results`/`max_credits`/`credits_used`/`results`), `checkpoint`
  (last completed operation), and one `sources[]` entry per source with
  `canonical_url`, `title`, `author`, `date`, `license`, `content_type`,
  `bytes`, `sha256`, `raw_path`, `status` and `error`/`retries`;
- `provenance.md` — a human-readable sidecar;
- `raw/<slug>.md` — the captured payload of each source.

Source status is a closed set: `discovered` (found, not acquired) → `fetched`
(payload stored and hashed) → `parsed`/`verified`; `rejected` is a terminal
failure. A source is never promoted past a status the CLI could not check.

### Archive layout

`fetch` writes only under the run's transient `context/tmp/research/<run-id>/raw/`.
The approved promotion (`populate-wiki --apply --confirm`) archives each verified
capture into a **dated, objective-scoped directory** of `context/refs/`:

```
context/refs/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<research-objective>/<result>.md
```

- the `<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>` stamp is the run's `created` instant, so the directory
  is unique by construction and two runs on one objective stay separate;
- `<research-objective>` is the run's objective slug (`--objective`, else derived
  from the query, else `research`);
- `<result>` is the source title slug (URL last-segment fallback), with a
  `-<sha256[:8]>` suffix only when another capture in the run yields the same
  name.

The archive refuses a pre-existing directory that is not this run's own (a
foreign collision) without writing, records `archive_dir` in the manifest, and
lets the promoted wiki page cite `refs/<dir>/<result>.md@<sha>`. The legacy flat
`refs/<slug>-<hash8>.md` captures are left untouched.

### Run commands

- `sdt research init --query "<q>" [--scope "<s>"] [--objective "<kebab-slug>"]`
  — create a run; `--objective` names the archive directory and is derived from
  the query when omitted.
- `sdt research search --query "<q>" --network --confirm [--limit N]
  [--max-credits N] [--api-url <url>]` — discover sources with the configured
  provider (Firecrawl). **Search is opt-in**: it refuses to run without
  `--network` and `--confirm` because it reaches the network and spends
  provider credits. The key comes from `FIRECRAWL_API_KEY` (shell or the
  project `.env`); only discovery happens here.
- `sdt research fetch <url>...` (or no URL = every `discovered` source) —
  acquire pages with the **crawldown pipeline** (Markdown + provenance: title,
  date, content type, size, SHA-256). Content acquisition is always crawldown,
  never another engine (HARD RULE 10).
- `sdt research inspect [--run <id>]` — read-only view of sources, status,
  budget and checkpoint.
- `sdt research verify [--run <id>]` — deterministically recompute the SHA-256,
  check the payload and schema, and mark each source `verified`/`rejected` with
  a reason. It never upgrades a source it cannot check; it exits non-zero when
  any checked source is rejected.
- `sdt research resume [--run <id>]` — continue from the checkpoint: fetch the
  still-`discovered` sources, verify what has a payload, skip finished work. It
  is idempotent — replaying a completed run repeats nothing.
- `sdt research synthesize [--out <path>] [--include-unverified]
  [--max-lines N]` — assemble a **cited draft** from verified sources (no model,
  no network). Unverified sources are excluded unless requested, then flagged;
  the draft is written only under `context/tmp/` or to a `*.draft.md` file.
- `sdt research populate-wiki --brief "<why>" [--max-pages N] [--apply
  --confirm]` — propose and, only on approval, apply the promotion of verified
  findings to the wiki. Preview is the default and writes nothing.

### Policy

- **Budget before the network.** Declare results/credits bounds; the run refuses
  to start when the remaining credits are below `--max-credits`.
- **Provenance is data, not prose.** Every source carries URL, hash, size and
  the raw path; a claim cites its source.
- **Checkpoints.** Each stage records its completion; an interrupted run resumes
  without repeating completed work.
- **Promotion is separate and gated.** A verified run is evidence; a wiki page is
  curated knowledge. `populate-wiki` previews, then applies only on `--confirm`,
  archives each raw capture into the run's dated directory under
  `context/refs/<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS>-<research-objective>/` and cites it, then
  reindexes and runs the wiki lint. A page is never created from an unverified
  claim; conflicts become relations or open questions.
- **Negatives are kept.** A failed fetch keeps its error and retry count and
  stays retryable; rejected sources stay recorded.

### From a run to a note

When a run concludes, write the durable `context/research/<date>-<slug>.md` note
(above): summarise the findings, list the evidence under `sources` (the archived
`refs/` captures), and link forward to the analysis/proposal/wiki that consumes
it. Keep findings atomic and citable (claim + source + scope + date). Prefer
`refs/` for immutable raw captures; the note summarises and links, it does not
duplicate the payload.
